---
import LandingPage from "../layouts/LandingPage.astro";
import { articleJsonLd, faqJsonLd } from "../lib/seo";
import { APP_URL } from "../lib/config";

const UPDATED = "2026-08-18";
const canonical = "/study-with-me";
const title = "Study Together Online: Live Study Rooms for Exam Prep";
const description =
  "Study together online in a live study room — not a YouTube video and not the Study Together app. Quiet, timed sessions with a real partner for UPSC, JEE, NEET, and other long grinds.";

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "What does study together online mean here?",
    answer:
      "It means a live, timed study room with a real person — not a recording, and not a branded 24/7 lobby. You join a session, share what you'll study, and work in parallel. That's 'study with me' without the livestream performance.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is this the Study Together app?",
    answer:
      "No. Study Together is a separate product with always-on group rooms. Refocus is 1:1 matched sessions: 25, 50, or 75 minutes, one partner, quiet mics. If you searched 'study together' and wanted a live partner for this block, you're in the right place.",
  },
  {
    question: "Why is studying alone so hard?",
    answer:
      "Long prep cycles like UPSC, JEE, or NEET are isolating, and isolation makes procrastination easier and motivation harder to sustain. Studying with someone present adds gentle accountability and structure, so you sit down and actually begin — which is usually the hardest part.",
  },
  {
    question: "How is an online study room different from a study group?",
    answer:
      "A study group discusses material together. An online study room is quiet and parallel — everyone does their own work with someone present. That keeps sessions focused instead of turning into a discussion, which is better for solo syllabus grinding.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do I need to talk during a study session?",
    answer:
      "No. You share one line about what you'll study, then work quietly against a shared timer. Cameras stay on for presence; mics stay quiet. The point is co-presence, not conversation.",
  },
];

const article = articleJsonLd({
  headline: title,
  description,
  path: canonical,
  dateModified: UPDATED,
});
---

<LandingPage
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  eyebrow="Guide"
  heading="Study together online: live study rooms for exam prep"
  lead="Solo exam prep is a grind, and the loneliness is half the battle. An online study room lets you study alongside someone else in real time — quiet, timed, and accountable — so you actually sit down and start."
  updated={UPDATED}
  faqs={faqs}
  ctaTitle="Study with someone tonight"
  ctaText="Refocus is a virtual study room: pick a session length, get matched with a study partner who's also studying, and work side by side with a shared timer. No talking, just company."
  ctaHref={`${APP_URL}/auth/sign-up`}
  ctaLabel="Start a free session"
  jsonLd={[article, faqJsonLd(faqs)]}
>
  <h2>From "study with me" videos to live study rooms</h2>
  <p>
    "Study with me" videos work because watching someone else focus nudges you
    to focus too. But a recording can't tell whether you actually studied. A live
    <strong>online study room</strong> closes that gap: you join a timed session
    and study in parallel with a real person who's also preparing for something.
    It's the study-specific form of
    <a href="/body-doubling">body doubling</a> — the presence of a working
    partner makes it far easier to begin.
  </p>

  <h2>Study together online — without the Study Together lobby</h2>
  <p>
    "Study together" is also the name of a popular always-on group room app.
    This page is not that product. If you want to
    <strong>study together online</strong> as in: one partner, a shared timer,
    and a quiet block for UPSC, JEE, NEET, or CA, that's what Refocus runs.
    Group lofi lobbies can wait. Your syllabus can't. For the room metaphor
    itself, see <a href="/focus-room">online focus room</a>; for the person,
    see <a href="/study-partner">quiet study partner</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Why solo prep breaks people (and how co-study helps)</h2>
  <p>
    Multi-month prep for exams like UPSC, JEE, NEET, CA, or GATE is isolating by
    design — most of it happens alone at a desk. Isolation quietly erodes
    consistency: it's easier to skip a day, and easier to spiral after a bad
    mock test. Studying with someone present rebuilds structure and accountability
    without adding pressure. We've written specifically about
    <a href="/blog/combatting-study-isolation">beating study isolation</a> and
    <a href="/blog/handle-bad-scores-jee-neet-mock-tests">handling bad mock scores</a>
    if either of those is your sticking point.
  </p>

  <h2>How to run a good study session</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Decide the one topic or problem set before you sit down.</li>
    <li>Pick a session length that matches your energy (25, 50, or 75 minutes).</li>
    <li>Put your phone out of reach; keep the shared timer in view.</li>
    <li>At the end, log what you covered — small wins compound over months.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    For more exam-specific tactics — active recall, night-owl scheduling,
    restarting after a wasted day — browse our
    <a href="/blog/category/exams">competitive exam prep articles</a>. And when
    you're ready to stop studying alone, Refocus matches you with a
    <a href="/study-partner">study partner</a> automatically, so there's no
    schedule to coordinate.
  </p>
</LandingPage>
